1
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,284
Tuscumbia, Alabama, April 5th, 1887.

2
00:00:10,690 --> 00:00:12,362
Behind an old farmhouse,

3
00:00:12,478 --> 00:00:16,054
a young woman pumped cold water
over the hand of a little girl.

4
00:00:16,542 --> 00:00:20,199
Into the girl's other hand, she
spelled a word with her fingers.

5
00:00:20,396 --> 00:00:23,438
W, A, T, E, R.

6
00:00:23,856 --> 00:00:26,944
Then she spelled it again, faster.

7
00:00:27,394 --> 00:00:29,228
The girl was six years old.

8
00:00:29,507 --> 00:00:30,622
She could not see.

9
00:00:30,819 --> 00:00:31,992
She could not hear.

10
00:00:32,584 --> 00:00:36,624
For almost five years, she had
lived in a world without light,

11
00:00:36,798 --> 00:00:39,317
without sound, and without words.

12
00:00:39,817 --> 00:00:42,951
She had kicked, screamed,
and broken things,

13
00:00:43,056 --> 00:00:46,713
because she had no other
way to say what she wanted.

14
00:00:47,163 --> 00:00:50,286
At that pump, she suddenly
understood that the movements

15
00:00:50,344 --> 00:00:54,013
in her hand meant the cool
thing pouring over her skin.

16
00:00:54,628 --> 00:00:56,161
Everything had a name.

17
00:00:56,776 --> 00:01:01,071
Later she wrote, "That
living word awakened my soul,

18
00:01:01,524 --> 00:01:06,435
gave it light, hope, joy, set it free."

19
00:01:06,886 --> 00:01:09,812
That girl would learn to
read in five languages,

20
00:01:10,021 --> 00:01:12,877
graduate from one of the
best colleges in America,

21
00:01:13,121 --> 00:01:17,765
write more than a dozen books, and
travel to almost forty countries.

22
00:01:18,275 --> 00:01:23,221
She would meet every American president
from Grover Cleveland to Lyndon Johnson.

23
00:01:23,732 --> 00:01:26,657
She would also fight
for workers, for women,

24
00:01:26,936 --> 00:01:30,024
and for millions of blind
people around the world.

25
00:01:30,465 --> 00:01:32,787
Her name was Helen Keller.

26
00:01:33,484 --> 00:01:37,675
Today, we follow her life
from beginning to end.

27
00:01:42,076 --> 00:01:46,383
Helen Adams Keller was
born on June 27th, 1880,

28
00:01:46,766 --> 00:01:50,064
in Tuscumbia, a small
town in northern Alabama.

29
00:01:50,505 --> 00:01:53,396
Her family lived on a
farm called Ivy Green.

30
00:01:53,999 --> 00:01:56,403
The main house was a simple white home,

31
00:01:56,646 --> 00:02:01,441
surrounded by English ivy,
roses, and honeysuckle.

32
00:02:01,891 --> 00:02:03,830
Her father, Arthur Keller,

33
00:02:04,004 --> 00:02:07,441
had been a captain in the Confederate
army during the Civil War.

34
00:02:08,044 --> 00:02:12,549
After the war, he ran a cotton farm
and edited the local newspaper.

35
00:02:13,153 --> 00:02:16,961
Her mother, Kate, was almost twenty
years younger than her husband.

36
00:02:17,565 --> 00:02:21,164
She was educated, proud, and determined.

37
00:02:21,614 --> 00:02:23,855
Helen was their first child together,

38
00:02:24,157 --> 00:02:27,326
and by every account, a
bright and lively baby.

39
00:02:27,965 --> 00:02:29,962
She walked on the day she turned one.

40
00:02:30,368 --> 00:02:35,035
Before she was a year and a half old,
she had already begun to say a few words.

41
00:02:35,511 --> 00:02:38,518
One of them was "water."

42
00:02:42,918 --> 00:02:47,272
In February 1882, when Helen
was nineteen months old,

43
00:02:47,585 --> 00:02:49,698
she fell sick with a high fever.

44
00:02:50,383 --> 00:02:54,644
Doctors at the time called it a
"congestion of the stomach and brain."

45
00:02:55,074 --> 00:02:59,764
Today, many believe it was
scarlet fever or meningitis.

46
00:03:00,193 --> 00:03:03,583
For days, her parents
thought she would die.

47
00:03:04,034 --> 00:03:07,505
Then, one morning, the fever was gone.

48
00:03:08,156 --> 00:03:10,257
Her mother was overjoyed.

49
00:03:10,907 --> 00:03:14,146
But a few days later, she
noticed something strange.

50
00:03:14,622 --> 00:03:17,989
When the dinner bell rang,
Helen did not turn her head.

51
00:03:18,546 --> 00:03:23,202
When her mother passed a hand in front
of her eyes, Helen did not blink.

52
00:03:23,899 --> 00:03:29,623
The illness had left her completely
blind and completely deaf.

53
00:03:30,072 --> 00:03:34,043
As she grew, Helen made up her
own signs to talk with her family.

54
00:03:34,519 --> 00:03:36,318
A push meant "go."

55
00:03:36,550 --> 00:03:38,245
A pull meant "come."

56
00:03:38,896 --> 00:03:40,660
Shivering meant "cold."

57
00:03:41,310 --> 00:03:45,304
To ask for her father, she pretended
to put on a pair of glasses.

58
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:49,205
By the age of five, she
had about sixty signs.

59
00:03:49,588 --> 00:03:51,573
But she knew there was something more.

60
00:03:52,084 --> 00:03:56,299
She would stand between people who were
talking and touch their moving lips.

61
00:03:56,995 --> 00:04:00,037
She moved her own lips,
and nothing happened.

62
00:04:00,548 --> 00:04:03,648
Then she would fly into a rage.

63
00:04:04,098 --> 00:04:06,710
Her tantrums grew worse every year.

64
00:04:07,407 --> 00:04:10,681
She grabbed food from other
people's plates at the dinner table.

65
00:04:11,285 --> 00:04:15,000
She once locked her mother in
the pantry and sat on the porch,

66
00:04:15,325 --> 00:04:18,204
feeling the door shake as
her mother pounded on it.

67
00:04:19,016 --> 00:04:23,277
When her baby sister Mildred was
born, Helen tipped over the cradle.

68
00:04:23,881 --> 00:04:28,989
Some relatives quietly said that the
child should be sent to an asylum.

69
00:04:33,390 --> 00:04:35,538
Kate Keller refused to give up.

70
00:04:36,142 --> 00:04:39,915
One day, she read a book by the
English writer Charles Dickens,

71
00:04:39,996 --> 00:04:41,610
called American Notes.

72
00:04:42,306 --> 00:04:48,390
In it, Dickens described a girl named Laura
Bridgman, who was both blind and deaf.

73
00:04:49,087 --> 00:04:55,217
Fifty years earlier, at a school in Boston,
Laura had learned to read and write.

74
00:04:55,667 --> 00:05:00,706
In 1886, the Kellers took Helen to
a famous eye doctor in Baltimore.

75
00:05:01,217 --> 00:05:03,272
He could do nothing for her eyes.

76
00:05:03,597 --> 00:05:07,881
But he told them to see a man in
Washington who worked with deaf children.

77
00:05:08,392 --> 00:05:13,454
That man was Alexander Graham Bell,
the inventor of the telephone.

78
00:05:13,903 --> 00:05:15,726
Bell was kind to Helen.

79
00:05:16,422 --> 00:05:18,988
He let her feel his pocket watch ring.

80
00:05:19,685 --> 00:05:22,854
Years later, she said that
he had opened the door

81
00:05:22,936 --> 00:05:26,291
through which she would pass
from darkness into light.

82
00:05:27,104 --> 00:05:31,817
Bell told her father to write to the
Perkins Institution for the Blind in Boston,

83
00:05:32,421 --> 00:05:35,869
the same school where Laura
Bridgman had been taught.

84
00:05:36,319 --> 00:05:40,382
The director of Perkins, Michael
Anagnos, read the letter.

85
00:05:40,893 --> 00:05:42,763
He had a former student in mind.

86
00:05:43,273 --> 00:05:44,934
She was twenty years old,

87
00:05:45,108 --> 00:05:49,903
she had just graduated at the top
of her class, and she had no job.

88
00:05:50,506 --> 00:05:53,130
Her name was Anne Sullivan.

89
00:05:57,530 --> 00:06:00,792
Anne Sullivan's own life had
been a story of hardship.

90
00:06:01,292 --> 00:06:07,050
She was born in 1866 in Massachusetts,
to poor Irish immigrants.

91
00:06:07,654 --> 00:06:12,855
When she was five, an eye disease called
trachoma began to destroy her sight.

92
00:06:13,459 --> 00:06:15,920
When she was eight, her mother died.

93
00:06:16,524 --> 00:06:20,936
Her father, a heavy drinker,
soon abandoned the family.

94
00:06:21,386 --> 00:06:24,126
At ten, Annie and her
little brother Jimmie

95
00:06:24,172 --> 00:06:26,819
were sent to the state
poorhouse in Tewksbury.

96
00:06:27,261 --> 00:06:32,996
It was a crowded, filthy place, full
of the sick, the old, and the insane.

97
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:38,232
Jimmie had a bad hip, and only a
few months later, he died there.

98
00:06:39,044 --> 00:06:41,308
Annie was left alone.

99
00:06:41,759 --> 00:06:44,487
She stayed in the
poorhouse for four years.

100
00:06:44,870 --> 00:06:49,294
Then, in 1880, a group of state
officials came to inspect it.

101
00:06:49,990 --> 00:06:54,460
Annie threw herself in front of them
and begged to be sent to school.

102
00:06:55,064 --> 00:06:58,953
A few months later, at fourteen,
she arrived at Perkins.

103
00:06:59,557 --> 00:07:05,025
She could not read or write, and she did
not even know how to spell her own name.

104
00:07:05,475 --> 00:07:08,737
At Perkins, surgeons operated on her eyes,

105
00:07:09,062 --> 00:07:11,663
and her sight improved
enough for her to read.

106
00:07:12,267 --> 00:07:15,367
She was quick, stubborn,
and often in trouble.

107
00:07:16,063 --> 00:07:21,102
But in June 1886, she gave the
graduation speech for her class.

108
00:07:21,799 --> 00:07:27,024
Months later, she packed her bag
and took the train south to Alabama.

109
00:07:31,423 --> 00:07:36,264
Anne Sullivan arrived at Ivy
Green on March 3rd, 1887.

110
00:07:36,706 --> 00:07:39,306
Helen later called it her soul's birthday.

111
00:07:39,910 --> 00:07:41,640
Helen was standing on the porch.

112
00:07:42,081 --> 00:07:44,089
She felt footsteps coming toward her,

113
00:07:44,415 --> 00:07:48,327
and reached out her hand,
thinking it was her mother.

114
00:07:48,777 --> 00:07:54,292
The next morning, Annie gave Helen a doll,
a gift from the blind girls at Perkins.

115
00:07:54,803 --> 00:07:57,984
Then she spelled the word
"doll" into her hand.

116
00:07:58,680 --> 00:08:01,049
Helen copied the movements of the fingers.

117
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:06,273
She was proud of herself, but she had
no idea that she was spelling a word.

118
00:08:06,970 --> 00:08:09,814
To her, it was only a game.

119
00:08:10,265 --> 00:08:12,680
The real battle came
at the breakfast table.

120
00:08:13,005 --> 00:08:16,174
Helen walked around, grabbing
food from every plate.

121
00:08:16,685 --> 00:08:20,598
When she reached for Annie's
plate, Annie refused to let her.

122
00:08:21,109 --> 00:08:25,196
Helen threw herself on the
floor, kicking and screaming.

123
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:29,642
Annie asked the family to leave
the room, and locked the door.

124
00:08:30,246 --> 00:08:32,870
For the next hour, they fought.

125
00:08:33,381 --> 00:08:40,219
In the end, Helen sat in her chair, ate
with a spoon, and folded her napkin.

126
00:08:40,668 --> 00:08:43,896
Annie realized that she
could not teach Helen as

127
00:08:43,977 --> 00:08:47,100
long as her parents gave
in to her every wish.

128
00:08:47,704 --> 00:08:50,293
She asked for Helen to
live with her alone.

129
00:08:50,896 --> 00:08:55,076
The family let them move into a
small cottage near the main house.

130
00:08:55,680 --> 00:09:00,649
For two weeks, Annie spelled words
into Helen's hand all day long.

131
00:09:01,345 --> 00:09:02,715
Helen calmed down.

132
00:09:03,319 --> 00:09:05,293
She learned to trust her teacher.

133
00:09:05,896 --> 00:09:09,925
But she still did not
understand what words were.

134
00:09:14,326 --> 00:09:17,821
On the morning of April 5th, 1887,

135
00:09:18,204 --> 00:09:23,951
Annie and Helen argued over
two words, "mug" and "water."

136
00:09:24,636 --> 00:09:26,249
Helen kept confusing them.

137
00:09:26,691 --> 00:09:31,602
In anger, she threw her new doll
on the floor and smashed it.

138
00:09:32,051 --> 00:09:35,604
Annie took her outside, to the
water pump near the well house.

139
00:09:36,207 --> 00:09:41,281
Someone was pumping water, and Annie
held Helen's hand under the cold stream.

140
00:09:41,978 --> 00:09:49,234
Into her other hand, she spelled the
word "water," first slowly, then quickly.

141
00:09:49,684 --> 00:09:51,298
Helen stood very still.

142
00:09:51,820 --> 00:09:53,817
Suddenly, she understood.

143
00:09:54,351 --> 00:09:59,564
The word in her hand was the name of the
wonderful cool thing flowing over her skin.

144
00:10:00,098 --> 00:10:01,619
Everything had a name.

145
00:10:02,234 --> 00:10:06,171
She dropped to the ground, touched
it, and asked for its name.

146
00:10:06,867 --> 00:10:09,421
She touched the pump, and the fence.

147
00:10:10,118 --> 00:10:14,449
Then she turned and touched Annie,
and Annie spelled "teacher."

148
00:10:15,261 --> 00:10:19,406
For the rest of her life,
Helen called her by that name.

149
00:10:19,856 --> 00:10:23,664
By that evening, Helen had
learned thirty new words.

150
00:10:24,268 --> 00:10:28,540
That night, she went back to the
pieces of the broken doll and tried,

151
00:10:28,714 --> 00:10:31,454
without success, to put them together.

152
00:10:32,151 --> 00:10:36,784
For the first time in her life, she
felt sorry for what she had done.

153
00:10:37,225 --> 00:10:40,557
Within a few months, she
knew hundreds of words,

154
00:10:40,801 --> 00:10:44,980
and she was learning to read
raised letters and Braille.

155
00:10:49,380 --> 00:10:51,447
Michael Anagnos was thrilled.

156
00:10:51,830 --> 00:10:56,752
In his reports, he wrote about the
little girl from Alabama as a miracle.

157
00:10:57,263 --> 00:11:00,897
Newspapers across America and
Europe picked up the story.

158
00:11:01,222 --> 00:11:04,612
By the age of eight, Helen was famous.

159
00:11:05,062 --> 00:11:11,378
In 1888, Helen and Annie traveled to
Boston, and Helen began studying at Perkins.

160
00:11:11,889 --> 00:11:16,115
For the first time, she could talk with
children who knew the finger alphabet.

161
00:11:16,556 --> 00:11:19,215
She read every book she
could find in Braille.

162
00:11:19,598 --> 00:11:23,174
She was learning French, Latin, and Greek.

163
00:11:23,623 --> 00:11:26,920
At Perkins, Helen also met Laura Bridgman,

164
00:11:27,245 --> 00:11:30,299
the woman whose story had
first given her mother hope.

165
00:11:30,902 --> 00:11:35,221
Laura was now almost sixty, and
she still lived at the school.

166
00:11:35,825 --> 00:11:39,366
The two deafblind women spelled
into each other's hands.

167
00:11:39,807 --> 00:11:41,943
But Laura was stiff and strict,

168
00:11:42,268 --> 00:11:45,890
and she scolded the excited
little girl for being too rough.

169
00:11:46,587 --> 00:11:50,418
It was a strange meeting
between the past and the future.

170
00:11:50,869 --> 00:11:54,004
But Helen wanted more than
spelling with her hands.

171
00:11:54,329 --> 00:11:55,629
She wanted to speak.

172
00:11:56,233 --> 00:12:01,829
In 1890, at the age of nine, she began
taking lessons from Sarah Fuller,

173
00:12:02,270 --> 00:12:04,871
the head of a school
for the deaf in Boston.

174
00:12:05,683 --> 00:12:08,052
Helen put her hand on her teacher's face,

175
00:12:08,563 --> 00:12:14,321
feeling how her tongue and lips
moved, and how her throat vibrated.

176
00:12:14,771 --> 00:12:21,331
After only eleven lessons, she said her
first full sentence: "I am not dumb now."

177
00:12:21,934 --> 00:12:25,023
For the rest of her life,
she would practice speaking.

178
00:12:25,719 --> 00:12:31,013
Her voice always sounded unusual, and
strangers often found it hard to follow her.

179
00:12:31,524 --> 00:12:34,229
But she never stopped trying.

180
00:12:38,630 --> 00:12:43,332
In the fall of 1891, eleven-year-old
Helen wrote a short story

181
00:12:43,425 --> 00:12:48,591
called "The Frost King" and sent it
to Anagnos as a birthday present.

182
00:12:49,033 --> 00:12:52,516
He loved it, and had it
printed in a magazine.

183
00:12:52,965 --> 00:12:56,947
Soon, someone noticed that it was
very similar to a story called

184
00:12:57,226 --> 00:13:01,591
"The Frost Fairies," published
years before Helen was born.

185
00:13:02,195 --> 00:13:05,504
It seems that someone had
once read that story to Helen,

186
00:13:05,887 --> 00:13:08,081
and that it had stayed in her memory.

187
00:13:08,778 --> 00:13:12,052
She was certain she
had made it up herself.

188
00:13:12,502 --> 00:13:14,627
Perkins held a kind of trial.

189
00:13:15,068 --> 00:13:20,165
A group of teachers questioned the child
for two hours, without Annie in the room.

190
00:13:20,675 --> 00:13:23,566
In the end, the vote was split evenly,

191
00:13:23,775 --> 00:13:27,583
and Anagnos cast the deciding
vote in Helen's favor.

192
00:13:28,094 --> 00:13:30,230
But their friendship was broken.

193
00:13:31,043 --> 00:13:33,516
Helen cried herself to sleep that night.

194
00:13:33,899 --> 00:13:37,301
For years afterward, she was
afraid to write anything,

195
00:13:37,626 --> 00:13:40,273
in case the words were not her own.

196
00:13:44,673 --> 00:13:48,365
In 1894, Helen and
Annie moved to New York,

197
00:13:48,609 --> 00:13:51,000
where Helen studied at
a school for the deaf.

198
00:13:51,697 --> 00:13:53,764
There, at the age of fourteen,

199
00:13:54,007 --> 00:13:58,187
she met one of the most famous
writers in the world, Mark Twain.

200
00:13:58,884 --> 00:14:02,634
He treated her not as a
curiosity, but as a friend.

201
00:14:03,330 --> 00:14:07,893
He told her stories, and she
read his lips with her fingers.

202
00:14:08,343 --> 00:14:09,922
Helen had a dream.

203
00:14:10,526 --> 00:14:14,531
She wanted to go to college,
just like other young women.

204
00:14:14,972 --> 00:14:20,057
But her father had died in 1896,
and the family had little money.

205
00:14:20,754 --> 00:14:26,187
Twain asked a rich friend, the oil
businessman Henry Rogers, to help.

206
00:14:26,884 --> 00:14:30,634
Rogers and his wife agreed
to pay for her education.

207
00:14:31,084 --> 00:14:34,904
Twain once compared Annie
Sullivan to a miracle worker.

208
00:14:35,507 --> 00:14:38,213
He is said to have called
Helen and Napoleon the two

209
00:14:38,294 --> 00:14:41,510
most interesting characters
of the nineteenth century.

210
00:14:42,113 --> 00:14:47,988
Their friendship lasted until his
death, sixteen years after they met.

211
00:14:52,388 --> 00:14:53,758
To get into college,

212
00:14:53,932 --> 00:14:57,903
Helen first went to a preparatory
school in Cambridge, Massachusetts.

213
00:14:58,344 --> 00:15:03,127
In 1899, she passed the entrance
exams for Radcliffe College,

214
00:15:03,452 --> 00:15:05,763
the women's college of Harvard University.

215
00:15:06,367 --> 00:15:09,571
The next year, she
began her classes there.

216
00:15:10,021 --> 00:15:11,298
College was hard.

217
00:15:11,577 --> 00:15:13,806
Few of her books were printed in Braille.

218
00:15:14,131 --> 00:15:19,088
In every lecture, Annie sat beside her
and spelled everything into her hand,

219
00:15:19,263 --> 00:15:21,538
as fast as the professor could speak.

220
00:15:22,049 --> 00:15:28,132
At night, Annie read the books to her,
page after page, ruining her own weak eyes.

221
00:15:28,643 --> 00:15:31,662
Helen typed her essays on a typewriter.

222
00:15:32,112 --> 00:15:36,175
While she was still a student, she
wrote the story of her own life.

223
00:15:36,779 --> 00:15:40,982
It was published in 1903
as The Story of My Life.

224
00:15:41,493 --> 00:15:48,041
It became a bestseller, and is still
read today, in more than fifty languages.

225
00:15:48,491 --> 00:15:53,681
In June 1904, Helen Keller graduated
from Radcliffe with honors.

226
00:15:54,064 --> 00:15:55,747
She was twenty-four years old.

227
00:15:56,130 --> 00:16:00,275
She became the first deafblind
person ever to earn a college degree.

228
00:16:00,972 --> 00:16:06,649
Annie sat beside her at the ceremony,
but the diploma bore only Helen's name.

229
00:16:11,049 --> 00:16:14,126
People often asked Helen how
she knew the world around her.

230
00:16:14,729 --> 00:16:18,027
The answer was through
touch, smell, and taste.

231
00:16:18,630 --> 00:16:21,777
She recognized her friends
by the shape of their hands,

232
00:16:22,102 --> 00:16:25,190
and could tell their mood
by the way they shook hers.

233
00:16:25,700 --> 00:16:28,417
She knew when a door slammed
somewhere in the house,

234
00:16:28,626 --> 00:16:31,947
because she felt the floor
tremble under her feet.

235
00:16:32,397 --> 00:16:34,069
To follow what people said,

236
00:16:34,278 --> 00:16:36,275
she often placed her
thumb on the speaker's

237
00:16:36,333 --> 00:16:39,456
lips and her fingers
along the jaw and throat.

238
00:16:40,152 --> 00:16:43,461
In this way, she could
understand a conversation,

239
00:16:43,844 --> 00:16:48,233
slowly, without anyone
spelling into her hand.

240
00:16:48,683 --> 00:16:51,017
Her sense of smell was remarkable.

241
00:16:51,620 --> 00:16:53,675
She said she could often tell what work a

242
00:16:53,745 --> 00:16:56,125
person did from the
scent of their clothes.

243
00:16:56,729 --> 00:17:02,708
A carpenter smelled of wood, a painter
of paint, and an iron worker of metal.

244
00:17:03,311 --> 00:17:07,247
She could smell a storm coming,
hours before it arrived.

245
00:17:07,851 --> 00:17:13,853
In 1908, she described all of this
in a book called The World I Live In.

246
00:17:14,303 --> 00:17:16,219
She loved being outdoors.

247
00:17:16,544 --> 00:17:20,642
She swam, rowed a boat, rode
a bicycle built for two,

248
00:17:20,886 --> 00:17:24,636
and played chess and checkers on
boards made especially for her.

249
00:17:25,449 --> 00:17:28,362
As a child, she had written
that the best and most

250
00:17:28,432 --> 00:17:31,996
beautiful things in the world
cannot be seen or even touched.

251
00:17:32,600 --> 00:17:35,758
They must be felt with the heart.

252
00:17:40,159 --> 00:17:44,722
After college, Helen and Annie bought
a farmhouse in Wrentham, Massachusetts.

253
00:17:45,105 --> 00:17:48,472
In 1905, Annie married John Macy,

254
00:17:48,716 --> 00:17:51,966
a young Harvard teacher who
had helped Helen edit her book.

255
00:17:52,570 --> 00:17:55,113
The three of them lived together.

256
00:17:55,562 --> 00:17:58,697
Helen began to read about
the causes of blindness.

257
00:17:59,138 --> 00:18:03,886
She learned that many poor people lost
their sight because of dangerous factories,

258
00:18:04,037 --> 00:18:06,290
bad housing, and disease.

259
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:10,411
She came to believe that
poverty itself was the enemy.

260
00:18:11,224 --> 00:18:14,927
In 1909, she joined the Socialist Party.

261
00:18:15,378 --> 00:18:17,758
She spoke and wrote for workers' rights,

262
00:18:17,932 --> 00:18:21,160
for votes for women,
and against child labor.

263
00:18:21,671 --> 00:18:24,631
She opposed America entering
the First World War.

264
00:18:25,142 --> 00:18:28,637
She would later help found the
American Civil Liberties Union.

265
00:18:29,450 --> 00:18:31,226
Many Americans were shocked.

266
00:18:31,737 --> 00:18:35,150
The newspapers that had once
called her a saint now said

267
00:18:35,196 --> 00:18:39,202
that her opinions came from
the limits of her disabilities.

268
00:18:39,651 --> 00:18:42,089
One editor wrote that
her mistakes came from

269
00:18:42,159 --> 00:18:45,584
the "manifest limitations
of her development."

270
00:18:46,187 --> 00:18:47,732
Helen answered sharply.

271
00:18:48,173 --> 00:18:53,107
When she had met him years before, she
wrote, he had praised her intelligence.

272
00:18:53,548 --> 00:18:55,859
Now that she held opinions he disliked,

273
00:18:56,184 --> 00:18:59,365
he suddenly remembered that
she was blind and deaf.

274
00:18:59,876 --> 00:19:04,427
It was his newspaper, she said,
that was socially blind and deaf.

275
00:19:05,031 --> 00:19:09,326
The FBI would later keep a file
on her political activities.

276
00:19:13,726 --> 00:19:18,974
By 1914, Annie's marriage had fallen
apart, and John Macy moved out.

277
00:19:19,577 --> 00:19:21,110
Annie's health was failing.

278
00:19:21,621 --> 00:19:26,172
A young Scottish woman named Polly
Thomson came to work as Helen's secretary.

279
00:19:26,613 --> 00:19:30,444
She would stay with Helen
for almost fifty years.

280
00:19:30,895 --> 00:19:33,577
In 1916, while Annie was ill,

281
00:19:33,751 --> 00:19:38,511
a twenty-nine-year-old man named Peter
Fagan was hired as Helen's secretary.

282
00:19:39,022 --> 00:19:42,168
He was kind and clever,
and they fell in love.

283
00:19:42,772 --> 00:19:45,407
They secretly applied
for a marriage license.

284
00:19:45,918 --> 00:19:48,658
But a newspaper found out
and printed the story.

285
00:19:49,262 --> 00:19:51,154
Helen's mother was furious.

286
00:19:51,595 --> 00:19:55,798
Her family chased Peter away,
and the plan to marry collapsed.

287
00:19:56,402 --> 00:19:57,737
Helen never married.

288
00:19:58,341 --> 00:20:02,915
Later she wrote that this brief love
had been "a little island of joy,

289
00:20:03,158 --> 00:20:05,631
surrounded by dark waters."

290
00:20:06,082 --> 00:20:07,940
Money was a constant worry.

291
00:20:08,172 --> 00:20:14,221
In 1919, Helen starred in a Hollywood film
about her own life, called Deliverance.

292
00:20:14,430 --> 00:20:16,218
It failed at the box office.

293
00:20:16,821 --> 00:20:21,709
So, from 1920, Helen and Annie
went on the vaudeville stage.

294
00:20:22,394 --> 00:20:26,620
Between jugglers and comedians,
Annie told the story of their life,

295
00:20:26,945 --> 00:20:29,557
and Helen answered
questions from the audience.

296
00:20:30,161 --> 00:20:33,342
Asked once whether she closed
her eyes when she slept,

297
00:20:33,946 --> 00:20:37,104
she replied that she
never stayed awake to see.

298
00:20:37,801 --> 00:20:43,652
For four years, they toured the country,
and earned more than ever before.

299
00:20:48,052 --> 00:20:50,896
In 1915, during the First World War,

300
00:20:51,105 --> 00:20:55,900
Helen helped found an organization to help
soldiers who had been blinded in battle.

301
00:20:56,411 --> 00:21:00,707
It still exists today, under the
name Helen Keller International,

302
00:21:00,985 --> 00:21:05,142
and works to prevent blindness
and hunger in many countries.

303
00:21:05,592 --> 00:21:11,025
In 1924, Helen began working for the
new American Foundation for the Blind.

304
00:21:11,467 --> 00:21:14,543
She would work for it for
more than forty years.

305
00:21:14,984 --> 00:21:20,929
She gave speeches across America, raised
millions of dollars, and spoke to Congress.

306
00:21:21,533 --> 00:21:25,457
She helped persuade the government
to pay for books for blind readers,

307
00:21:25,840 --> 00:21:29,091
and to support blind
people who needed help.

308
00:21:29,540 --> 00:21:33,209
That same year, she wrote a
famous letter about music.

309
00:21:33,812 --> 00:21:36,460
She had placed her hand
on a radio while an

310
00:21:36,529 --> 00:21:39,548
orchestra played
Beethoven's Ninth Symphony.

311
00:21:40,059 --> 00:21:44,888
Through the vibrations, she said, she
could feel the different instruments,

312
00:21:45,062 --> 00:21:49,416
and the voices of the choir
rising and falling like a flame.

313
00:21:49,867 --> 00:21:54,174
In 1933, the Nazis in Germany
burned books they hated.

314
00:21:54,778 --> 00:21:58,505
One of them was a collection of
Helen's essays about socialism.

315
00:21:59,201 --> 00:22:01,930
She wrote an open letter
to the students of Germany.

316
00:22:02,533 --> 00:22:07,166
"You can burn my books and the books of
the best minds in Europe," she wrote,

317
00:22:07,491 --> 00:22:10,939
"but the ideas in them have
seeped through a million channels,

318
00:22:11,264 --> 00:22:14,480
and will continue to quicken other minds."

319
00:22:18,880 --> 00:22:23,211
By the early 1930s, Annie
Sullivan was nearly blind herself.

320
00:22:23,536 --> 00:22:25,997
She was often in pain, and tired.

321
00:22:26,601 --> 00:22:31,790
Helen and Polly cared for her, as
Annie had once cared for Helen.

322
00:22:32,240 --> 00:22:39,067
Anne Sullivan died on October 20th, 1936,
in New York, with Helen holding her hand.

323
00:22:39,578 --> 00:22:41,133
She was seventy years old.

324
00:22:41,574 --> 00:22:45,510
For almost fifty years, the two
women had hardly ever been apart.

325
00:22:46,207 --> 00:22:49,772
Her ashes were placed at the
Washington National Cathedral.

326
00:22:50,375 --> 00:22:54,671
She was the first woman honored
there for her own achievements.

327
00:22:55,120 --> 00:22:56,827
Helen was fifty-six.

328
00:22:57,430 --> 00:23:01,935
She said that she felt as if she had
lost her eyes and ears a second time.

329
00:23:02,632 --> 00:23:04,013
For the rest of her life,

330
00:23:04,292 --> 00:23:10,074
she would never stop speaking about
the woman she simply called "Teacher."

331
00:23:14,474 --> 00:23:18,990
Only a few months after Annie's death,
Helen and Polly sailed for Japan.

332
00:23:19,594 --> 00:23:21,486
Annie had once promised to go with her.

333
00:23:21,928 --> 00:23:23,797
Helen kept the promise alone.

334
00:23:24,401 --> 00:23:27,303
For three months, she
traveled across the country,

335
00:23:27,477 --> 00:23:32,121
gave nearly a hundred speeches,
and was welcomed by huge crowds.

336
00:23:32,571 --> 00:23:39,119
In July 1937, she crossed the sea to
Korea, which was then under Japanese rule.

337
00:23:39,618 --> 00:23:45,377
For one week, she traveled from
Busan to Daegu, Seoul, and Pyongyang.

338
00:23:45,888 --> 00:23:49,928
In Seoul, she spoke in a large public
hall in the center of the city,

339
00:23:50,253 --> 00:23:52,111
and the tickets sold out.

340
00:23:52,807 --> 00:23:55,733
Korean newspapers covered her every step.

341
00:23:56,337 --> 00:23:59,889
Historians now note that the
Japanese colonial authorities

342
00:24:00,214 --> 00:24:04,290
also tried to use her visit
for their own purposes.

343
00:24:04,740 --> 00:24:08,107
In Japan, Helen heard
the story of Hachiko,

344
00:24:08,432 --> 00:24:11,218
the loyal Akita dog who
had waited at Shibuya

345
00:24:11,288 --> 00:24:15,096
station for his dead master
for almost ten years.

346
00:24:15,607 --> 00:24:18,033
She asked for an Akita of her own.

347
00:24:18,417 --> 00:24:22,039
A police officer gave her
a puppy named Kamikaze-go.

348
00:24:22,549 --> 00:24:25,777
He became the first Akita
ever brought to America.

349
00:24:26,381 --> 00:24:29,005
Sadly, he died only months later.

350
00:24:29,608 --> 00:24:34,368
The Japanese government then
sent her his brother, Kenzan-go.

351
00:24:38,768 --> 00:24:43,749
In 1939, Helen and Polly moved into
a new home in Easton, Connecticut.

352
00:24:44,190 --> 00:24:46,233
She named it Arcan Ridge.

353
00:24:46,837 --> 00:24:49,496
Every morning, she
walked alone along a long

354
00:24:49,565 --> 00:24:52,746
rail that had been built
for her in the garden.

355
00:24:53,197 --> 00:24:54,880
During the Second World War,

356
00:24:55,124 --> 00:25:00,024
Helen visited wounded American soldiers
in military hospitals across the country.

357
00:25:00,720 --> 00:25:04,029
Many had lost their sight
or their hearing in battle.

358
00:25:04,633 --> 00:25:07,628
She sat beside them, touched their faces,

359
00:25:07,872 --> 00:25:11,308
and told them that their
lives were not over.

360
00:25:11,758 --> 00:25:17,517
In 1946, while Helen was traveling in
Europe, Arcan Ridge burned to the ground.

361
00:25:18,120 --> 00:25:20,558
Almost everything she owned was lost,

362
00:25:20,802 --> 00:25:24,215
including her library of
Braille books and the manuscript

363
00:25:24,262 --> 00:25:27,292
of a book she had been
writing about Annie for years.

364
00:25:27,617 --> 00:25:29,544
She started again from the beginning.

365
00:25:29,928 --> 00:25:32,424
Friends helped rebuild the house.

366
00:25:32,874 --> 00:25:38,133
After the war, she began traveling the
world for blind people in poorer countries.

367
00:25:38,830 --> 00:25:44,008
Over eleven years, she visited
thirty-five countries on five continents.

368
00:25:44,612 --> 00:25:47,828
In 1948, she went back to Japan,

369
00:25:48,106 --> 00:25:51,868
and walked through the ruins
of Hiroshima and Nagasaki.

370
00:25:52,379 --> 00:25:55,722
She was nearly seventy years old.

371
00:26:00,123 --> 00:26:04,871
In 1955, Helen finally
published her book about Annie.

372
00:26:05,475 --> 00:26:07,937
It was called simply Teacher.

373
00:26:08,540 --> 00:26:11,269
The same year, a
documentary about her life,

374
00:26:11,594 --> 00:26:16,423
Helen Keller in Her Story,
won an Academy Award.

375
00:26:16,873 --> 00:26:20,948
Two years later, a writer named
William Gibson turned the story

376
00:26:21,006 --> 00:26:25,313
of Helen and Annie into a
television play, The Miracle Worker.

377
00:26:25,917 --> 00:26:28,959
In 1959, it opened on Broadway,

378
00:26:29,168 --> 00:26:34,776
with Anne Bancroft as Annie and a young
actress named Patty Duke as Helen.

379
00:26:35,380 --> 00:26:39,176
Every night, the audience watched
the fight at the breakfast table,

380
00:26:39,455 --> 00:26:42,508
and the moment at the water pump.

381
00:26:42,958 --> 00:26:45,942
In 1962, it became a film.

382
00:26:46,453 --> 00:26:49,297
Both actresses won Academy Awards.

383
00:26:49,901 --> 00:26:52,606
A new generation of people
around the world came to

384
00:26:52,664 --> 00:26:56,286
know the story of the
little girl and her teacher.

385
00:27:00,686 --> 00:27:03,600
In 1960, Polly Thomson died.

386
00:27:03,844 --> 00:27:09,161
Helen was now eighty, and had lost the last
of the two women who had been her eyes,

387
00:27:09,231 --> 00:27:12,528
her ears, and her voice
for most of her life.

388
00:27:13,341 --> 00:27:16,801
A nurse named Winnie
Corbally took care of her.

389
00:27:17,251 --> 00:27:20,978
In 1961, Helen suffered
a series of strokes.

390
00:27:21,361 --> 00:27:26,632
She retired from public life, and spent
her last years quietly at Arcan Ridge,

391
00:27:26,876 --> 00:27:29,441
reading, and receiving
visits from friends.

392
00:27:30,045 --> 00:27:36,117
In 1964, President Lyndon Johnson awarded
her the Presidential Medal of Freedom,

393
00:27:36,442 --> 00:27:40,378
the highest honor an American
civilian can receive.

394
00:27:40,828 --> 00:27:46,772
Helen Keller died in her sleep on
June 1st, 1968, at Arcan Ridge.

395
00:27:47,376 --> 00:27:49,152
She was eighty-seven years old,

396
00:27:49,361 --> 00:27:51,892
just a few weeks short of
her eighty-eighth birthday.

397
00:27:52,589 --> 00:27:56,037
Her ashes were placed at the
Washington National Cathedral,

398
00:27:56,281 --> 00:28:00,008
beside those of Anne
Sullivan and Polly Thomson.

399
00:28:00,457 --> 00:28:02,640
Her story did not end with her death.

400
00:28:02,965 --> 00:28:07,516
In 2003, the state of Alabama put
her image on its quarter coin,

401
00:28:07,725 --> 00:28:09,640
with her name written in Braille.

402
00:28:10,244 --> 00:28:14,574
It was the first American coin in
general circulation to carry Braille.

403
00:28:15,526 --> 00:28:19,172
In 2009, a bronze statue
of Helen was placed

404
00:28:19,218 --> 00:28:21,540
in the United States
Capitol in Washington.

405
00:28:22,144 --> 00:28:24,303
It shows her not as an old woman,

406
00:28:24,629 --> 00:28:28,692
but as a seven-year-old girl,
standing at the water pump.

407
00:28:29,143 --> 00:28:33,543
She had been called a saint,
a miracle, and a radical.

408
00:28:34,240 --> 00:28:38,501
For much of her life, people loved
the little girl at the water pump,

409
00:28:38,826 --> 00:28:41,937
but did not want to hear what
the grown woman had to say.

410
00:28:42,378 --> 00:28:46,244
She spoke anyway, about
poverty, about war,

411
00:28:46,395 --> 00:28:48,903
and about the rights of women and workers.

412
00:28:49,600 --> 00:28:53,233
She showed the world what people
with disabilities could do,

413
00:28:53,477 --> 00:28:55,753
if they were only given the chance.

414
00:28:56,203 --> 00:29:00,650
The little girl who once had no
words left behind millions of them.

415
00:29:01,253 --> 00:29:06,234
As she once wrote, "Life is either
a daring adventure, or nothing."

